  "question": {
    "text": "A body of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;is moving in a straight line with momentum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi></math>. Starting at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, a force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></math> acts in the same direction on the moving particle during time interval of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math>. So that its momentum changes from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> is",
    "image": null
  },
